  "body": "**A flag stays a flag, and `q` quits before the dashboard too.** Two things that did not do what the docs and the screen said: a command-line flag was written to your config file by the next save about anything else, and on the launch screen `q` could be taken by a panel you could not see.\n\n## A flag is for the run it is given to\n\nThe guide says \"Flags override the config file for that run\". They also reached the file. Measured on 0.41.0, against a config with `theme = \"octoscope\"`, `refresh_interval = \"60s\"`, `compact = false` and `show_sponsor = true`:\n\n```\noctoscope --theme amber --refresh 5m --compact --no-sponsor\n```\n\nthen `p` twice (public-only on and off again) and `q`. The file then read `amber`, `5m0s`, `true` and `false`. Pinning a repository or saving the settings panel did the same, and `--public-only` went the same way.\n\nA config write now leaves a setting the run took from a flag as the file had it. A setting you change in the app is a choice and is saved: a row of the settings panel whose value you changed, or `p`. Closes [#260](https://github.com/gfazioli/octoscope/issues/260). ([#263](https://github.com/gfazioli/octoscope/pull/263))\n\nOne thing that may read as a change: if you used a flag once to set something for good, it no longer sticks. Change that row in the settings panel (`,`) instead.\n\n## `q` quits on the launch screen\n\nBefore the first dashboard octoscope draws the launch screen, which shows one key: `q quit`. Nothing that draws on the dashboard is on screen there, and the keys that open those things still worked:\n\n| On the launch screen, 0.41.0 | What happened |\n| --- | --- |\n| `,` then `q` | the settings panel opened behind the screen and took the `q`: no quit |\n| a list tab chosen ahead, `/`, then `q` | a filter box opened and the `q` was typed into it |\n| `p` | public-only switched and the config was written, with nothing on screen to say so |\n\nUntil there is a dashboard the screen now takes `q`, `ctrl+c`, the two overlays it can draw (`?` and `%`) and a tab chosen ahead (`1`–`8`, `tab`), which is where the dashboard opens. Every other key does nothing. This is 0.41.0's error-screen rule, taken to the screen before it. Closes [#261](https://github.com/gfazioli/octoscope/issues/261). ([#264](https://github.com/gfazioli/octoscope/pull/264))\n\nWhat is given up: a filter typed before the first dashboard arrives is no longer kept.\n\n## Notable polish\n\n- **The release's own Homebrew check passes again.** v0.41.0's post-release cask check went red on a cask that installs: it turned Homebrew's JSON API off, which on the current CI image sent the cask's one formula dependency through a six-minute clone and then did not find it. The check now installs the way a Mac does. Installs and upgrades were never affected. Closes [#259](https://github.com/gfazioli/octoscope/issues/259). ([#262](https://github.com/gfazioli/octoscope/pull/262))\n- **README and the flags guide** say it in so many words: a flag is never saved, a setting changed in the app is.\n\n## Tests\n\n- **Flags.** Every flag and then an unrelated config write; the same values read from the file, which are written as before; one flag at a time; `p` twice; and the settings panel saved with nothing changed, with each row changed alone, and with every row changed.\n- **The launch screen.** 15 keys that must do nothing there (no command, no surface, no tab, sort or public-only change, no config write, no fetch) and `q` quitting after each; the tab keys and the dashboard arriving on the tab chosen; `?`, `%`, `ctrl+c`; and the same keys back once the dashboard is on screen.\n- **Mutation-tested.** 20 mutants over the flags and 9 over the launch screen: every one fails a test.\n- **In the real binary**: the measured flag sequence leaves the file as it was, and `,` then `q` on the launch screen quits, where 0.41.0 kept running.\n- **Reviewed by Codex**, one pass per pull request. Every finding was verified against the code before it was applied.\n\n## Upgrade\n\n```\nbrew upgrade gfazioli/tap/octoscope\n```\n"
